// case: capitalised-type-annotation
// what: `Int` is not a type. `mapTypeToC` has cases for int, float, string,
//      bool, void, list, map, result and response, and for any other name whose
//      first character is upper case it answers `sanitizeIdentifier(name) + "*"`
//      (`compiler/c_backend.orb:264`, case test at :293-300). So the annotation
//      is accepted, carried through the whole pipeline as a string, and written
//      into the C as `Int* r_0` -- and the C step is what reports it. The front
//      end that has the annotation in front of it says nothing.
//
//      The part that hides this: it only fails if the binding is READ. Delete
//      the `print` and the identical program builds and runs, because an unused
//      local is never declared in the generated C, so the invented type is
//      never written down. Verified both ways:
//
//        val n: Int = 1  print(n)  -> check clean, gcc: unknown type name 'Int'
//        val n: Int = 1             -> check clean, builds, runs
//
//      That is why this reads as "works" in a test and breaks in an
//      application. FINDINGS F-0011.
// known-defect: F-0011
// cc-rejects: unknown type name
fn main() -> int {
    val n: Int = 1
    print(n)
    return 0
}
